---
import Essay from '../layouts/Essay.astro';
---

<Essay
  docNo="03"
  title="How a product creates value"
  description="A view of product value as a continuous conversation: understanding the problem behind a request, making it buildable, and learning from real use."
  thesis="Product development starts with the problem behind a request. It turns that understanding into a product, then keeps learning from the people who use it. Value is the continuing ability to solve a problem that matters."
>
  <div class="sub">
    <div class="sub__body">
      <p>
        Following products from design through NPI, mass production, and sustaining changed how I
        understand value.
      </p>
      <p>
        A product creates value when development stays connected to the problem that matters, from
        the first conversation through real use.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <section class="sub">
    <div class="sub__head">
      <span class="sub__no">3.1</span>
      <h2>A request is only the beginning</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="sub__body">
      <p>
        Customers do not arrive with requirements. They describe what gets in the way, ask for a
        feature, or explain what they are trying to accomplish.
      </p>
      <p>
        A requested feature is already one interpretation of the problem. Product development starts
        by listening past the proposed solution: what is the customer trying to do, why is it
        difficult now, and what would make the outcome meaningfully better?
      </p>
      <div class="pullquote">
        <p>The request is the beginning. The problem behind it is the work.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sub">
    <div class="sub__head">
      <span class="sub__no">3.2</span>
      <h2>Understanding has to become buildable</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="sub__body">
      <p>
        A customer problem cannot go straight to a drawing or a factory. It has to become
        requirements specific enough for design, firmware, manufacturing, reliability, and the rest
        of the team to act on.
      </p>
      <p>
        Those requirements meet reality throughout development. Performance, quality, cost, and
        schedule all change the solution. The work is to keep those changes connected to the problem
        they were meant to solve.
      </p>
      <p>
        Completing a product does not mean preserving the first solution. It means building one that
        can be repeated, delivered, and supported without losing the reason it exists.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sub">
    <div class="sub__head">
      <span class="sub__no">3.3</span>
      <h2>Use continues the conversation</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="sub__body">
      <p>
        Once the product is in use, the conversation changes. Feedback, real behaviour, returns, and
        changing expectations reveal what the requirements captured and what they missed.
      </p>
      <p>
        Sustaining closes that loop. It corrects the product, sharpens the team's understanding, and
        informs what should be built next. The product may need to change. The understanding of the
        problem may change with it.
      </p>
      <div class="pullquote">
        <p>
          A valuable product keeps solving a problem that matters, even as the product and the need
          evolve.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Essay>
